    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d584">

                <pb facs="0787" n="697" sameAs="frus:frus1920v02/pg697"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.73/465</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Third Assistant Secretary of
                        State</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Merle-Smith</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1920-12-20"><hi rendition="#italic">December 20,
                        1920</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Mr. Vallance</hi>: I telephoned the British Embassy
                    Saturday and talked to Mr. Tennant.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d584-f1"/> I informed
                    him of the situation, telling him that we must at least have some security that
                    the cable would not be laid within the territorial waters of the United States
                    to avoid embarrassing complications; that the best thing to do would be to have
                    the cable taken off the vessel in England. Hp said he agreed with me that this
                    should be done and would telegraph London <pb facs="0788" n="698" xml:id="pg698"
                    /> in regard to the matter, and that if they could not have that done they would
                    try and arrange for some assurance which would satisfy us.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Van</hi> S. M[<hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">erle</hi>]-S[<hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >mith</hi>]</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d584-f1" n="1" xml:id="d584-f1-content">
                <p>Mr. H. V. Tennant, Secretary, temporarily attached to the British Embassy.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
